---
/**
 * The zero-cost path, placed after the affiliate disclosure on purpose: these
 * two links earn nothing, and sitting below the disclosure makes that visible
 * rather than something the reader has to take on trust.
 *
 * rel is deliberately just noopener — no `sponsored`, unlike the hardware links.
 */

const apps = [
  {
    name: 'Linphone',
    url: 'https://www.linphone.org/en/',
    cost: 'Free · open source',
    where: 'iOS · Android · macOS · Windows · Linux',
    note:
      'Runs on everything, including the desktop you are reading this on. The quickest way to hear the lobby answer without owning a single handset.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Groundwire',
    url: 'https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=cz.acrobits.softphone.aliengroundwire&hl=en-US',
    cost: 'Paid, around $10',
    where: 'Android · also on iOS',
    note:
      'Acrobits, and worth the money for one reason: it works much harder to stay reachable in the background. That is the difference between an app you open and a phone that rings.',
  },
];
---

<div class="softphones">
  <p class="eyebrow">Nothing to buy</p>
  <h3 class="display softphones-head">Not ready for a dedicated handset?</h3>

  <p class="softphones-lede">
    A softphone is a real SIP endpoint. It registers exactly like the hardware
    above, gets its own entry in <code>handsets.toml</code>, and rings when the
    ladder says so &mdash; so you can hear the bouncer, test a ringer ladder, and
    dial your own extension using the phone already in your pocket, before
    spending anything.
  </p>

  <ul class="softphone-list">
    {
      apps.map((a) => (
        <li>
          <a href={a.url} rel="noopener">{a.name}</a>
          <span class="softphone-meta">{a.cost}</span>
          <span class="softphone-where">{a.where}</span>
          <p class="softphone-note">{a.note}</p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p class="softphones-catch">
    <strong>The honest catch.</strong> A softphone only rings while it is
    registered, and phone operating systems are ruthless about suspending apps in
    the background. On a desktop that is a non-issue. On a mobile it needs push
    notifications or a foreground service, and push against a self-hosted PBX
    takes real configuration. Treat a softphone as the best way to try all of
    this, and as a genuinely good desk phone &mdash; not yet as the handset you
    expect to wake you at 2am. That is what the hardware above is for.
  </p>
</div>
